Now flip the ledger and trace how thoroughly Scheme's lasting ideas conquered modern programming! Look at a four-line cross-language snapshot showing Scheme's DNA thriving inside today's mainstream code:

```rust
// JavaScript (Lisp-1 lexical closure):
const makeAdder = (n) => (x) => x + n;
// Rust (expression-oriented block returning a value):
let badge = if score > 90 { "gold" } else { "silver" };
```

Every corner of modern software bears Scheme's imprint. JavaScript normalized first-class closures and Lisp-1 naming for millions of web developers. Python—which famously *lacked* nested lexical scoping in its earliest releases—adopted full lexical closures in Python 2.1–2.2 alongside anonymous `lambda` functions. And Rust went even further, adopting lexical closures, hygienic `macro_rules!` inspired directly by Scheme's `syntax-rules`, and **expression-oriented design**—the Scheme-descended style where `if` branches and code blocks evaluate directly to values (`"gold"` or `"silver"`) rather than merely running commands ('statements') that change variables without returning a value!
